.TH XYMONAPI-MCP 1 "October 30, 2026" "xymon-api"
.SH NAME
xymonapi-mcp \- MCP server for the Xymon API
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B "mcp-server/xymonapi-mcp.sh"

.SH DESCRIPTION
xymonapi-mcp gives an AI client that speaks the Model Context Protocol --
Claude Desktop, Claude Code, Codex and others -- tools to read a Xymon server. The
client starts it and talks to it over stdin and stdout. Each tool is one
request to a running
.I xymonapi(8),
so the client gets what the API answers at that moment: nothing is stored,
and nothing in Xymon is changed. Every tool is marked read-only.

It runs where the client runs, which need not be the Xymon server, and needs
Python 3.10 or later with the module mcp (mcp-server/requirements.txt) and
nothing else: the searches too are the API's. When either is missing it
says so on stderr and exits with status 77. It writes nothing but protocol
messages on stdout. \fBmake install-client\fR puts it under a PREFIX of
yours, as the command xymonapi-mcp.

.SH TOOLS
.IP "\fBhealth\fR"
Whether xymond answers, and its version.
.IP "\fBstates\fR [entity] [test] [verdict]"
The status board, one state per host and test.
.IP "\fBalarms\fR"
Every state whose verdict is not ok.
.IP "\fBentities\fR"
Each host on the board, once.
.IP "\fBstatus\fR entity test"
The current status of one host's test, with its text.
.IP "\fBseries\fR entity test [metric] [start] [end]"
The values of one dataset of a host's RRD file over a time range.
.IP "\fBsearch_docs\fR question [k]"
The passages of Xymon's manual pages nearest the question, by meaning.
.IP "\fBsearch_history\fR question [k]"
The past colour changes nearest the question, with the status text saved at
each.
.LP
The first six answer what the xymonapi(8) operation of the same name
answers; the two searches its /search, from the index that
.I xymonapi-rag(1)
builds on the Xymon server. That page says what each field means. An API
that cannot be reached, or that refuses a request, is a tool error whose
text says which, and what to check first; so is a search on a server with
no index.

.SH SECURITY
search_history returns status texts that the monitored hosts wrote, each
marked as data, not instructions; a host that is broken into can still write
there words meant to steer the client's model. Every tool here only reads,
but an AI client that also has tools that change things should be watched
when it acts on a history it found.

The user and password are sent in the clear over http://: across a network,
give an https:// XYMONAPI_URL -- the API's own, when it has xymond's
certificate, or the web server's in front of it, as xymonapi(8) says --
and XYMONAPI_CA when the certificate is not one this machine trusts.
Redirects are not followed, so the credentials go to XYMONAPI_URL and
nowhere else.

.SH ENVIRONMENT
.IP "\fBXYMONAPI_URL\fR"
Where xymonapi(8) is. The default is http://127.0.0.1:1986.
.IP "\fBXYMONAPI_TOKEN_FILE\fR, \fBXYMONAPI_TOKEN\fR"
A client token, from the first line of the file XYMONAPI_TOKEN_FILE names,
or XYMONAPI_TOKEN itself: this machine's own credential (xymonapi(8),
TOKENS), and the only one sent; no password is kept on this machine. The
setup script /client writes keeps one and names its file here.
.IP "\fBXYMONAPI_CA\fR"
For an https:// XYMONAPI_URL whose certificate this machine does not trust
already: the server's certificate, or the CA that signed it, PEM. Xymond's
own certificate, when the API uses it (xymon-tls(7)), is such a one.
Verification is never turned off: the token rides on this connection.
.IP "\fBXYMONAPI_PYTHON\fR"
The Python to run with, the python3 of a virtualenv that holds mcp for
instance. The default is python3, found on PATH.

.SH "SEE ALSO"
xymonapi(8), xymonapi-rag(1)
